WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.530
Good morning, Hank. It's Tuesday.

00:00:01.530 --> 00:00:06.809
So what if I told you that for Americans, a single data point can affect your life expectancy?

00:00:07.000 --> 00:00:11.920
More than whether you smoke, whether you get an annual physical, or whether you exercise regularly?

00:00:12.380 --> 00:00:17.920
That data point is your zip code, the five digit number the U.S. Postal Service uses to deliver your mail.

00:00:17.960 --> 00:00:21.940
In the U.S., your zip code can affect your life expectancy by 𝟐𝟎 𝐲𝐞𝐚𝐫𝐬,

00:00:21.980 --> 00:00:23.480
and according to some recent research,

00:00:23.480 --> 00:00:27.160
the gap between the healthiest communities and the sickest ones is getting bigger.

00:00:27.160 --> 00:00:28.620
So I live in the city of Indianapolis,

00:00:28.620 --> 00:00:31.320
and in my zip code, life expectancy is around 80,

00:00:31.320 --> 00:00:33.480
which is slightly higher than the U.S. average.

00:00:33.480 --> 00:00:35.300
But if you live three miles north of my house,

00:00:35.300 --> 00:00:38.580
average life expectancy rises to 83.7.

00:00:38.640 --> 00:00:43.600
And if you live three miles south of my house it falls to 69.4.

00:00:43.600 --> 00:00:45.280
Now maybe that doesn't seem like a big deal to you,

00:00:45.280 --> 00:00:48.760
but just to put it in some context, on average non-smokers in the U.S.

00:00:48.780 --> 00:00:50.960
live about ten years longer than smokers.

00:00:51.020 --> 00:00:55.300
People who exercise regularly live about 4.5 years longer than those who don't,

00:00:55.300 --> 00:00:58.160
and whether you live 3 miles north or south of my house

00:00:58.160 --> 00:01:00.980
affects your life expectancy by 14 years.

00:01:00.980 --> 00:01:05.680
Wait, slight tangent, am I going to spend all of those 4.5 extra years exercising?

00:01:05.680 --> 00:01:10.760
I have to do some math. Okay, so assuming 2.5 hours of moderate intensity exercise per week

00:01:10.760 --> 00:01:15.640
beginning at the age of sixteen, you're going to spend about one of your extra years exercising.

00:01:15.820 --> 00:01:18.020
Then you're going to spend another 1.5 years sleeping.

00:01:18.020 --> 00:01:22.280
You basically get two extra years, except of course, that's not how life expectancy works.

00:01:22.360 --> 00:01:27.340
If you, a particular individual, exercises, it doesn't mean that your particular life will be increased

00:01:27.340 --> 00:01:33.060
4.5 extra years, or even 4.5 extra hours. It just means that on average that's what happens.

00:01:33.060 --> 00:01:38.040
Similarly living in a particular zip code doesn't mean a particular fate for individuals, but on average

00:01:38.640 --> 00:01:42.140
geography has a huge impact on American health and longevity.

00:01:42.240 --> 00:01:44.720
So what's causing that profound life expectancy gap?

00:01:44.760 --> 00:01:46.500
Well as usual, it's complicated.

00:01:46.500 --> 00:01:49.500
Like the Eastern Kentucky communities where life expectancy is under 70

00:01:49.500 --> 00:01:51.640
have higher rates of tobacco usage than

00:01:51.640 --> 00:01:55.000
the central Colorado communities where life expectancy is over 85.

00:01:55.000 --> 00:01:58.340
But Eastern Kentucky is also poorer and has fewer doctors.

00:01:58.340 --> 00:02:03.020
Communities with low life expectancy tend to have less access to healthy food and more uninsured people.

00:02:03.020 --> 00:02:06.240
People in those communities are also more likely to have certain chronic health problems

00:02:06.240 --> 00:02:07.660
like diabetes and hypertension.

00:02:07.660 --> 00:02:10.920
Whereas the people in the healthiest communities tend to be...you know,

00:02:10.920 --> 00:02:13.220
rich, and have excellent access to health care.

00:02:13.220 --> 00:02:14.960
And the zip codes with the highest life expectancy

00:02:14.960 --> 00:02:19.260
also tend to be in places where there's lots of physical activity, like ski resorts in Colorado,

00:02:19.300 --> 00:02:23.220
or wealthy Beach towns in California. Now, I know to many of us, this is not surprising.

00:02:23.260 --> 00:02:27.380
Like, we take it for granted that rich people will have dramatically longer and healthier lives

00:02:27.380 --> 00:02:33.200
than poor people, but increasing inequality of life expectancy 𝐰𝐢𝐭𝐡𝐢𝐧 countries is not actually the norm.

00:02:33.200 --> 00:02:38.360
In fact while life expectancy variability has been rising within the U.S. steadily since 1980,

00:02:38.360 --> 00:02:40.860
in most other rich countries it's been declining.

00:02:40.960 --> 00:02:42.960
Canada for instance have seen a significant decline,

00:02:43.020 --> 00:02:47.400
which is one of the main reasons why Canada's overall life expectancy is higher than ours,

00:02:47.400 --> 00:02:53.380
even though the richest 20% of Americans live longer, on average, than the richest 20% of Canadians.

00:02:53.380 --> 00:02:53.940
SIDE NOTE:

00:02:53.940 --> 00:02:56.600
Lots of people take issue with life expectancy as a health metric,

00:02:56.600 --> 00:02:58.540
especially when comparing one country to another.

00:02:58.540 --> 00:02:59.960
And with some good reasons.

00:02:59.960 --> 00:03:02.720
But the data we're discussing here is mostly exempt from those critiques,

00:03:02.720 --> 00:03:05.100
much more info and sources in the doobly-Doo below.

00:03:05.120 --> 00:03:10.180
Right so this growing discrepancy in life expectancy is not normal, and it is not natural.

00:03:10.180 --> 00:03:14.940
We don't have to accept that lifespan will vary dramatically within a country based on where you live.

00:03:14.940 --> 00:03:18.440
But right now in the United States, that problem is getting worse.

00:03:18.440 --> 00:03:20.540
Not better. That seems to me deeply wrong.

00:03:20.640 --> 00:03:24.480
I mean our declaration of independence famously named three inalienable rights

00:03:24.480 --> 00:03:28.060
that Americans should enjoy, and before liberty and the pursuit of happiness,

00:03:28.060 --> 00:03:29.180
there is 𝐋𝐢𝐟𝐞.

00:03:29.180 --> 00:03:30.820
Hank, I'll see you on Friday.

00:03:30.820 --> 00:03:36.500
Actually no, I will see you tomorrow for a 𝐬𝐮𝐩𝐫𝐢𝐬𝐞 bonus vlogbrothers video, and then I'll see you on Friday.

